Cellular Respiration Cellular respiration is the process cells use to extract usable energy from nutrients such as glucose. 
In aerobic respiration, oxygen is used and carbon dioxide and water are produced. 
The main stages of cellular respiration are glycolysis, the citric acid cycle, and oxidative phosphorylation. Glycolysis occurs in the cytoplasm. 
During glycolysis, one glucose molecule is broken down into two molecules of pyruvate. A small amount of ATP and NADH is produced. 
The citric acid cycle occurs in the mitochondrial matrix in eukaryotic cells. It completes the breakdown of carbon compounds and produces ATP, NADH, and FADH2. 
Oxidative phosphorylation occurs at the inner mitochondrial membrane. Electrons from NADH and FADH2 move through an electron transport chain. 
The electron transport chain uses the energy released by electrons to pump hydrogen ions across the inner mitochondrial membrane. 
This creates a concentration gradient. ATP synthase uses the movement of hydrogen ions through the membrane to produce ATP. 
Oxygen serves as the final electron acceptor and combines with hydrogen ions to form water. 
Most ATP produced during aerobic cellular respiration comes from oxidative phosphorylation. 
When oxygen is unavailable, some cells can use fermentation to continue producing small amounts of ATP through glycolysis.
